This is a redacted excerpt from the full style guide. Platform-specific references have been generalized.
How to Use This Guide Section Overview 1. Content Taxonomy Framework for organizing solutions into domains, categories, and use cases 2. Naming Guidelines Naming conventions for each content type 3. Use Case Descriptions How to write customer-facing use case copy 4. Category Descriptions Guidelines for higher-level category descriptions 5. Solution Pages Tone, structure, and components for solution landing pages 6. Technical Guidance Architecture diagrams, narrative steps, and framework alignment 7. Writing Standards Stylistic elements, grammar principles, punctuation, and legal Content Taxonomy All content is organized into a hierarchy: Domain &gt; Category &gt; Use Case &gt; Solution Type. This taxonomy helps users navigate from broad business areas to specific solutions.
Mental Model Domain: The broadest organizational level (e.g., Financial Services, Healthcare, Analytics) Category: A portfolio of related use cases aligned to a discrete customer persona or technology area Use Case: A specific customer problem or opportunity Solution Type: The actual content asset (implementation guide, architecture diagram, deployment documentation) Naming Guidelines General Principles Use plain, descriptive text. No persuasive marketing copy, hyperbole, or unverifiable claims. Use standard industry terms familiar to the customer. Consider what a customer would search for. Use nouns, not verbs. Use title case. Separate multiple nouns with &ldquo;and&rdquo; rather than an ampersand (&quot;&amp;&quot;). By Content Type Content Type Format Character Limit Example Category Short title describing a set of related use cases using nouns ~50 characters Privacy-Enhanced Data Collaboration Use Case Short title describing a customer problem or opportunity using nouns ~50 characters Customer 360 Profile Solution &ldquo;[Use Case] on [Platform]&rdquo; or &ldquo;[Use Case] using [Service]&rdquo; ~55 characters Automated Data Analytics on Cloud Use Case Descriptions Use case descriptions appear on landing pages and should be 75-80 words maximum. They follow this structure:
1-2 sentences stating an existing challenge or opportunity for the customer 1 sentence introducing how the solutions address the challenge 1-2 sentences detailing business benefits (avoid getting too technical) Do NOT Include Customer names Negative or condescending language about the challenge Promises, assurances, or grand statements about what will be delivered Calls to action Jargon Writing Standards Voice and Tone The voice is direct, informative, and approachable. We avoid convoluted language and state the point upfront. We provide useful content, not fluff. Friendly rather than formal, but professional enough to maintain trust.
Conciseness Use fewer words and the right ones. Test: imagine you pay for each word. Are there any unnecessary words you can cut?
Do: &ldquo;Companies of all sizes want to predict future demand so they can improve the customer experience and increase revenue.&rdquo;
Don&rsquo;t: &ldquo;Big, medium, and small companies want to not only understand what their customers are looking for but also what they really need, so they must accurately and precisely predict future demand to improve and enhance revenue and make the customer experience better.&rdquo;
Weasel Words Avoid vague words that introduce ambiguity: should, could, would, might, some, many, most, few, often, occasionally.
Active Voice Use active voice (subject-verb-object). Passive voice is wordier and often obscures details.
Do: &ldquo;The streaming service sends data from social media feeds to the cloud.&rdquo;
Don&rsquo;t: &ldquo;Data is captured from social media feeds and sent to the cloud.&rdquo;
Inclusivity Don&rsquo;t use Use instead master primary, main, leader slave replica, secondary, follower whitelist allowlist, approved list blacklist denylist, blocklist Full guide is 7 sections with taxonomy rules, naming conventions, page component specifications, architecture diagram standards, and framework alignment guidelines. Available on request.
